WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.580
Cross-site scripting is browser-side injection: untrusted content is interpreted as executable script or unsafe markup.

2
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:23.480
Java web applications can produce browser output through Spring MVC views, Jakarta Servlet responses, JSP pages, server-rendered templates, REST endpoints consumed by frontend code, and JSON embedded into pages.

3
00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:30.940
The server may be written in Java, but the final interpreter is often the browser, and the browser has several different contexts.

4
00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:34.500
Output encoding must match the output context.

5
00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:45.480
Text inside normal HTML, values inside HTML attributes, URL components, CSS values, JavaScript strings, and JSON data all have different handling rules.

6
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:49.660
A value that is safe in one location may be unsafe in another.

7
00:00:49.760 --> 00:01:02.640
Autoescaping features in template engines are valuable, but developers need to know which contexts are covered, where escaping is disabled, and whether custom helpers or raw-output features bypass the normal protections.

8
00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:05.720
Sanitization is different from encoding.

9
00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:16.380
Sanitization attempts to allow some markup while removing unsafe parts, which is hard to do correctly and should rely on mature libraries and narrow requirements.

10
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:21.280
Encoding is usually preferred when the application only needs to display text.

11
00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:27.140
Client-side validation can improve usability, but it does not protect the server or other users.

12
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.360
The server must still validate input and encode output for the context where it is placed.

13
00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:39.280
Content Security Policy can provide defense in depth by limiting which scripts can execute and where resources may load from.

14
00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:47.220
It should not be the only control because policy gaps, legacy inline scripts, and unsafe rendering patterns can still create exposure.

15
00:01:47.320 --> 00:02:03.560
Strong Java web defense uses safe template defaults, context-specific output encoding, careful handling of rich text, review of raw output paths, and tests that verify untrusted content is displayed as content rather than interpreted by the browser.
